escription\": \"Optional allowlist of browser profile names exposed through node proxy routing. Leave empty to preserve the default full profile surface, including profile create/delete routes. When set, OpenClaw enforces least-privilege profile access and blocks persistent profile create/delete through the proxy.\"\n              \"description\": \"Configured model catalog and allowlist (keys are full provider/model IDs or literal provider/* entries for dynamic provider catalogs).\"\n              \"description\": \"Optional default skill allowlist inherited by agents that omit agents.list[].skills. Omit for unrestricted skills, set [] to give inheriting agents no skills, and remember explicit agents.list[].skills replaces this default instead of merging with it.\"\n                      \"description\": \"Optional CIDR allowlist for container-edge CDP ingress (for example 172.21.0.1/32).\"\n                \"description\": \"Optional allowlist of skills for this agent. If omitted, the agent inherits agents.defaults.skills when set; otherwise skills stay unrestricted. Set [] for no skills. An explicit list fully replaces inherited defaults instead of merging with them.\"\n                        \"description\": \"Per-agent override for CDP source CIDR allowlist.\"\n                    \"title\": \"Agent Tool Allowlist Additions\",\n                    \"description\": \"Per-agent additive allowlist for tools on top of global and profile policy. Keep narrow to avoid accidental privilege expansion on specialized agents.\"\n                          \"allowlist\",\n                            \"title\": \"Agent Message Action Allowlist\",\n                            \"description\": \"Per-agent message action allowlist for the message tool. Set to a minimal list such as [\\\"send\\\"] for public sandbox agents so read, edit, delete, reaction, and other provider-specific message actions stay hidden and blocked.\"\n          \"title\": \"Tool Allowlist\",\n          \"description\": \"Absolute tool allowlist that replaces profile-derived defaults for strict environments. Use this only when you intentionally run a tightly curated subset of tool capabilities.\"\n          \"title\": \"Tool Allowlist Additions\",\n          \"description\": \"Extra tool allowlist entries merged on top of the selected tool profile and default policy. Keep this list small and explicit so audits can quickly identify intentional policy exceptions.\"\n                      \"description\": \"Optional domain allowlist passed to the native Codex web_search tool.\"\n                  \"title\": \"Message Action Allowlist\",\n                  \"description\": \"Global message action allowlist for the message tool. Use only when the whole runtime should expose and accept a reduced action set; prefer per-agent allowlists for public or sandboxed agents.\"\n              \"title\": \"Agent-to-Agent Target Allowlist\",\n              \"description\": \"Allowlist of target agent IDs permitted for agent_to_agent calls when orchestration is enabled. Use explicit allowlists to avoid uncontrolled cross-agent call graphs.\"\n                \"allowlist\",\n              \"description\": \"Allow stdin-only safe binaries to run without explicit allowlist entries.\"\n              \"description\": \"Require explicit approval for interpreter inline-eval forms such as `python -c`, `node -e`, `ruby -e`, or `osascript -e`. Prevents silent allowlist reuse and downgrades allow-always to ask-each-time for those forms.\"\n                  \"title\": \"apply_patch Model Allowlist\","}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779340512290},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see the schema. Now I can see the `allowlist` field for agents under `tools`. Let me look more carefully at the config schema to understand the `tools.allowlist` structure for agents in 5.19.\n\nLooking at the output:\n- `agents.list[].tools.allowlist` - Per-agent additive allowlist for tools\n- `agents.list[].messageActionAllowlist` - Per-agent message action allowlist for the message tool\n\nWait, the user mentioned:\n> \"tools\": {\n>  \"allowlist\": [\"message\", \"channels\"]\n> }\n\nThis seems like a per-agent tools config. Let me check if this is about `agents.list[].tools.allowlist` or something else.\n\n\nI need to look more closely at the config schema to find where tools are structured at the agent level and whether there's a specific allowlist pattern I'm missing.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call0030808adeba46fa8fc454ccfc6ccf93","name":"process","arguments":{"action":"log","sessionId":"calm-tidepool"},"partialArgs":"{\"action\":\"log\",\"sessionId\":\"calm-tidepool\"}"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":30105,"output":219,"cacheRead":976,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31300,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779340512300,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ebb71773-38d2-4d7c-8f95-72233aa67664"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call0030808adeba46fa8fc454ccfc6ccf93","toolName":"process","content":[{"type":"text","text":"              \"type\": \"array\",\n              \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              \"title\": \"Browser Allowed Hostnames\",\n              \"description\": \"Explicit hostname allowlist exceptions for SSRF policy checks on browser/network requests. Keep this list minimal and review entries regularly to avoid stale broad access.\"\n            },\n            \"hostnameAllowlist\": {\n              \"type\": \"array\",\n              \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              \"title\": \"Browser Hostname Allowlist\",\n              \"description\": \"Legacy/alternate hostname allowlist field used by SSRF policy consumers for explicit host exceptions. Use stable exact hostnames and avoid wildcard-like broad patterns.\"\n            }\n          },\n          \"additionalProperties\": false,\n          \"title\": \"Browser SSRF Policy\",\n          \"description\": \"Server-side request forgery guardrail settings for browser/network fetch paths that could reach internal hosts. Keep restrictive defaults in production and open only explicitly approved targets.\"\n--\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"source\": {\n                    \"type\": \"string\",\n                    \"const\": \"env\"\n                  },\n                  \"allowlist\": {\n                    \"maxItems\": 256,\n                    \"type\": \"array\",\n                    \"items\": {\n                      \"type\": \"string\",\n                      \"pattern\": \"^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{0,127}$\"\n--\n          \"type\": \"array\",\n          \"items\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"title\": \"ACP Allowed Agents\",\n          \"description\": \"Allowlist of ACP target agent ids permitted for ACP runtime sessions. Empty means no additional allowlist restriction.\"\n        },\n        \"maxConcurrentSessions\": {\n          \"type\": \"integer\",\n          \"exclusiveMinimum\": 0,\n          \"maximum\": 9007199254740991,\n--\n              \"type\": \"array\",\n              \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"string\"\n              },\n              \"title\": \"Node Browser Proxy Allowed Profiles\",\n              \"description\": \"Optional allowlist of browser profile names exposed through node proxy routing. Leave empty to preserve the default full profile surface, including profile create/delete routes. When set, OpenClaw enforces least-privilege profile access and blocks persistent profile create/delete through the proxy.\"\n            }\n          },\n          \"additionalProperties\": false,\n          \"title\": \"Node Browser Proxy\",\n          \"description\": \"Groups browser-proxy settings for exposing local browser control through node routing. Enable only when remote node workflows need your local browser profiles.\"\n--\n                  }\n                },\n                \"additionalProperties\": false\n              },"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779340532893},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the structure better. Let me look at the specific section for the `message` and `channels` tool allowlist. Looking at the schema output, I can see there are fields like:\n\n1. `Agent Tool Allowlist Additions` - `agents.list[].tools.allowlist`\n2. `Agent Message Action Allowlist` - `agents.list[].tools.message.allowlist` \n3. `Message Action Allowlist` - `tools.message.allowlist` (global)\n\nThe user is asking about whether they need to add `tools: { allowlist: [\"message\", \"channels\"] }` for each agent after upgrading to 5.19.\n\n\nI'm trying to figure out if this is a required configuration change in 5.19 or if it's about restricting which message actions and channel integrations are available to","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"thinking","thinking":" each agent.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call5cbc7a8dfce24556901ddb0db0939a9f","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"openclaw config schema 2>/dev/null | python3 -c \"\nimport json,sys\nd=json.load(sys.stdin)\n# Find tools section\ndef find_tools(obj, path=''):\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\n        if 'tools' in obj.get('properties', {}):\n            print(f'Found tools at {path}')\n            print(json.dumps(obj['properties']['tools'], indent=2)[:3000])\n        for k,v in obj.items():\n            find_tools(v, path+'.'+k if path else k)\n    elif isinstance(obj, list) and len(obj) > 0:\n        find_tools(obj[0], path)\nfind_tools(d)\n\" 2>/dev/null | head -100"},"partialArgs":"{\"command\":\"openclaw config schema 2>/dev/null | python3 -c \\\"\\nimport json,sys\\nd=json.load(sys.stdin)\\n# Find tools section\\ndef find_tools(obj, path=''):\\n    if isinstance(obj, dict):\\n        if 'tools' in obj.get('properties', {}):\\n            print(f'Found tools at {path}')\\n            print(json.dumps(obj['properties']['tools'], indent=2)[:3000])\\n        for k,v in obj.items():\\n            find_tools(v, path+'.'+k if path else k)\\n    elif isinstance(obj, list) and len(obj) > 0:\\n        find_tools(obj[0], path)\\nfind_tools(d)\\n\\\" 2>/dev/null | head -100\"}"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":587,"output":344,"cacheRead":31072,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":32003,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779340532900,"responseId":"chatcmpl-f0ac783f-c25b-4670-a2d7-56f12e895248"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call5cbc7a8dfce24556901ddb0db0939a9f","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Command still running (session briny-comet, pid 540). Use process (list/poll/log/write/send-keys/submit/paste/kill/clear/remove) for follow-up."}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779340566160},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[assistant turn failed before producing content]"},{"type":"text","text":"[assistant turn failed before producing content]"},{"type":"text","text":"[assistant turn failed before producing content]"},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me wait for this and also check the current openclaw.json to see what agents are configured.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call911ed02b832a4843a71a35b25cc69b40","name":"process","arguments":{"timeout":15000,"action":"poll","sessionId":"briny-comet"},"partialArgs":"{\"timeout\":15000,\"action\":\"poll\",\"sessionId\":\"briny-comet\"}"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":31788,"output":70,"cacheRead":96,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31954,"cost":{"input":0